    "q-118720": {
      "dom_id": "step-q118720",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193755/assets/dd3401de484cfd0cb096c812d3f7540c31373116ed21b32bc9856b96c4b2ada3.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a regular pentagon and a regular octagon. Find the measure of the shaded angle.",
      "worked_solution": "The shaded angle is the sum of the measures of exterior angles of a regular pentagon and a regular octagon.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193755/assets/cd6a3ba04cc8f3f301b6db6a825854ba260cd5ce5b0582b64431f80c78cb7771.png)\n\nFor any polygon, the exterior angles sum up to ${360}^{∘}.$ In a regular pentagon, there are $5$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{5}={72}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, in a regular octagon, there are $8$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{8}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given angle has the following measure:\n\n$$\n{72}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}={117}^{∘}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
